# Messages gate — this project's seam for rasa.module.messages

This file is **project-owned**. `rasa.module.messages` copied it once at
install (`skip-if-exists`) and will never overwrite it. It holds the
judgement the module cannot make for you: **whose messages may be kept here,
what may be kept about other people and for how long, who the assistant may
write as, and what "needs a reply" means in this project.**

The machine half of the seam — your identities, the channels, services and
labels — lives in `messages/messages.config.yml`, because the tools check
it. The tools **hard-stop** until an identity is declared for a channel:
guessing who you may write as is the one inference this module refuses to
make. Declaring an identity makes writing *possible*; this file says whether
it is *allowed*.

The module records and drafts. It never sends anything; nothing here can
change that.

Fill in the sections. Honest defaults are shown; replace them.

---

## 1. Whose messages may be kept, and with what consent

Name whose conversations this project records, and on what basis. Examples:

- a household: the family's own email, texts and calls; nobody else's
  phone or mailbox.
- a firm: correspondence on a client matter, under the engagement letter;
  personal messages never.
- a clinic: messages with patients only as the clinic's records policy
  allows, and never a recording made without the other party's consent
  where the law requires it.

> _Default until you replace it: only messages the person running the
> assistant sent or received themselves, imported or recorded at their own
> request._

## 2. What may be kept about other people, and for how long

These records hold other people's words, numbers and addresses. Say what is
kept (whole messages? summaries? calls without notes?), what is never kept
(health details, account numbers, passwords sent by text, one-time codes),
and when a thread is archived or erased.

> _Default: keep messages whole; never keep one-time codes or passwords —
> discard such texts instead of importing them; archive threads that have
> been quiet for a year. Erasing personal data (a legal request, a
> relationship ended) is done deliberately, outside the tools, and noted
> here._

## 3. Who the assistant may write as

For each identity in `messages.config.yml`, say whether the assistant may
draft as it, and whether it may record a draft as sent (`mark-sent`) — which
should only ever follow a real send, done by a person, from their own
program.

| identity | may draft | may mark sent | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| _(none declared yet)_ | | | |

> _Default: the assistant may draft as any declared identity when asked;
> it marks a message sent only when the person says they sent it._

## 4. What "needs a reply" means here

The tools list a thread as needing a reply when the last thing that
happened in it came in. That is a starting list, not a verdict. Say what
does and does not count here, and how fast.

> _Default: newsletters, receipts, automated notices and one-time codes
> never need a reply. A missed call or a voicemail from a person does —
> within a day. Everything else from a person, within three days._

## 5. Hard gates (optional)

Anything that must be true before the assistant drafts or records a
message, which it must check and refuse without. Example (a firm):

> Before drafting to anyone outside the firm on a client matter, confirm
> the matter number and that the recipient is on the matter's contact list.
> Never draft as a partner's identity.

> _None declared._
